tion"],"description":"\u003cp\u003eWelcome to our guide to the beautiful and unique conservation areas in Kingston! Scroll through the presentation to learn about the different properties and choose your next recreational outing in town. Some properties are larger than others, with lots of trails and parking, and some are smaller without parking, so be sure to read through and plan accordingly. Happy trails!\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eNote: Several properties have not been added here yet for various reasons, mainly the need to improve access or trails. This includes the Evergreen Lots, Henrich, Kingston Shores Woodland, and Tree Farm Landing. These will be added shortly, we appreciate your patience!\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTrail maps for all properties can be found and downloaded at \u003c/p\u003e","tags":[""],"snippet":"Conservation lands owned \u0026 managed by the Town of Kingston's Conservation Commission","thumbnail":"thumbnail/thumbnail.jpeg","documentation":null,"extent":[],"categories":[],"spatialReference":null,"accessInformation":null,"classification":null,"licenseInfo":null,"culture":"en","properties":{"thumbnail":["url","https://su.example/content/items/1bbc593b25c54c5282d113f959992adb/resources/QRFEazd68TaxFVLrG5OVe.jpg{{qp|[[\"wago\"],[\"ust\"]]}}"],"shouldHideCover":false},"advancedSettings":null,"url":"https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/1bbc593b25c54c5282d113f959992adb","proxyFilter":null,"access":"public","size":232624766,"subInfo":0,"appCategories":[],"industries":[],"languages":[],"largeThumbnail":null,"banner":null,"screenshots":[],"listed":false,"commentsEnabled":true,"numComments":0,"numRatings":0,"avgRating":0,"numViews":2400,"scoreCompleteness":66,"groupDesignations":null,"apiToken1ExpirationDate":-1,"apiToken2ExpirationDate":-1,"lastViewed":1792454400000,"o":"ismb130npbS~m:\u003euhlN=Nn:smb127npb_W:okP}k]3[smb127npbX3iJZsmb127npbO9"},"publishedData":{"root":"n-7QYGwL","nodes":{"n-faWaC1":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-IpNwe6"}},"n-44FoPh":{"type":"storycover","data":{"type":"full","title":"Guide to Kingston Conservation Areas","summary":"Conservation lands owned \u0026 managed by the Town of Kingston's Conservation Commission","byline":"Created by Kingston Conservation Department staff","titlePanelVerticalPosition":"top","titlePanelHorizontalPosition":"center","titlePanelStyle":"gradient"},"config":{"isHidden":false},"children":["n-faWaC1"]},"n-fqxE39":{"type":"navigation","data":{"links":[{"nodeId":"n-AIYQRE"},{"nodeId":"n-2dEf1c"},{"nodeId":"n-cFEEpy"},{"nodeId":"n-ZHfJGB"},{"nodeId":"n-h6adul"},{"nodeId":"n-A3QrCR","customText":"F.S. Carter Jr. Preser..."},{"nodeId":"n-xemHyT"},{"nodeId":"n-f5VEOF","customText":"Hathaway Preserve"},{"nodeId":"n-gw77aJ","customText":"Marshall Joyce Sanctuary"},{"nodeId":"n-p68dp8"},{"nodeId":"n-ZNtC4P"},{"nodeId":"n-EphPrI","customText":"Sampson Park"},{"nodeId":"n-cbwHB7"},{"nodeId":"n-Iylaag"},{"nodeId":"n-qIWivp"}]},"config":{"isHidden":false}},"n-XMPf8J":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Welcome to our guide to the beautiful and unique conservation areas in Kingston! Scroll through the presentation to learn about the different properties and choose your next recreational outing in town. Some properties are larger than others, with lots of trails and parking, and some are smaller without parking, so be sure to read through and plan accordingly. Happy trails!","type":"large-paragraph"}},"n-8zsySE":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Note: Several properties have not been added here yet for various reasons, mainly the need to improve access or trails. This includes the Evergreen Lots, Henrich, Kingston Shores Woodland, and Tree Farm Landing. These will be added shortly, we appreciate your patience!","type":"paragraph"}},"n-RlLXXM":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Trail maps for all properties can be found and downloaded at \u003ca href=\"https://www.kingstonma.gov/600/Conservation-Properties-Trail-Maps\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.kingstonma.gov/600/Conservation-Properties-Trail-Maps\u003c/a\u003e","type":"large-paragraph"}},"n-6Eum7x":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"LAND ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: The Kingston Conservation Department acknowledges that the land we are charged with overseeing in Kingston is the territory of the Wampanoag Nation, the original stewards of this land. The Herring Pond Wampanoag Tribe in particular has existed in this area for thousands of years. We celebrate the Tribe having finally received state recognition, and look forward to a long partnership in healing the land. See \u003ca href=\"https://www.herringpondtribe.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.herringpondtribe.org/\u003c/a\u003e for more information.","type":"paragraph"}},"n-E0DYYC":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"paragraph"}},"n-kYgmsB":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"LAND STEWARDS PROGRAM","type":"h3"}},"n-0vSaoE":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Kingston Conservation Department and Commission utilizes a group of all-star volunteers to help manage and maintain our natural areas. Stewards sign up to walk specific trails once per month, then report back to the Conservation Office with any observed issues. Some stewards will then help us maintain properties through installation of signage and fencing, vegetative maintenance, trash removal, even woodworking. This program is crucial for the management of our conservation areas, and we are always looking for new volunteers! Please email Georgianna Minenna (\u003ca href=\"mailto:gminenna@kingstonma.gov\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003eGMinenna@kingstonma.gov\u003c/a\u003e, Assistant Conservation Agent, if you are interested in participating. See \u003ca href=\"https://www.kingstonma.gov/691/Kingston-Land-Steward-Program\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.kingstonma.gov/691/Kingston-Land-Steward-Program\u003c/a\u003e for details."}},"n-0CEp0Q":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-zwQLJn","caption":"QR code leading to our Land Stewards Program"}},"n-JX9okB":{"type":"webmap","data":{"map":"r-9e79d59a52b54111b9bbbe0354fe5d39","caption":"Kingston Conservation Land - zoom in to see property names","timeSlider":false,"extent":{"spatialReference":{"latestWkid":3857,"wkid":102100},"xmin":-7886860.343132611,"ymin":5150575.6792464005,"xmax":-7861655.233055608,"ymax":5164754.747993282},"center":{"spatialReference":{"latestWkid":3857,"wkid":102100},"x":-7874257.78809411,"y":5157665.213619841},"zoom":13,"viewpoint":{"rotation":0,"scale":72223.819286,"targetGeometry":{"spatialReference":{"latestWkid":3857,"wkid":102100},"x":-7874257.78809411,"y":5157665.213619841}}}},"n-AIYQRE":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Bay Farm","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-YCCjT0":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-fsPNWc":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 36.75 acres in Kingston, 80.75 acres including Duxbury.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eA large parking lot is located where Bay Road in Duxbury and Landing Road in Kingston meet. Access is also available on Bay Farm Road, but there is no parking at this entrance point. From that point, parking (with a Kingston town sticker) is available around the corner at the Kingston Harbormaster\u0026apos;s office, or at Mulliken\u0026apos;s Landing (there are no sidewalks between).\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory: \u003c/strong\u003eBay Farm is a collaborative 80-acre natural area between the Town of Kingston, Town of Duxbury, and Massachusetts Department of Conservation \u0026amp; Recreation (DCR). The Town of Kingston and DCR purchased their parcels in 1989, adding to the existing 44-acres in Duxbury. The land was formerly a dairy farm, but prioritized as open space in the 60\u0026apos;s due to the resulting population increase from the construction of Route 3. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-H7qHnN":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-AIYQRE","n-YCCjT0","n-fsPNWc"]},"n-2uv8V9":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-oCYDv4"}},"n-uLlUhk":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-H7qHnN","n-2uv8V9"]},"n-kfNVZb":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Recreation"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-nSLPYY":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Roughly 2.5-miles of trails exist at Bay Farm. The area is a popular spot for dog walking, but also botanizing, birding, and sightseeing. Kingston trails run east from the parking lot through the main fields, then through the wet forest, and to a smaller field by Bay Farm Road. Trails can often be wet, so wear appropriate shoes! Ice can make sections dangerous and impassable during cold spells in winter. The Cedar Rocks area on the Duxbury side provides great views of Kingston Bay. This is a great spot to scan the water for sea ducks in winter. The Duxbury fields have resident Bobolinks in the warm months, while the smaller fields in Kingston boast great floral diversity and are great for watching pollinators in action. Watch our social media feed for annual insect walks with Plymouth County Entomologist, Blake Dinius. Oh, and watch your step, as not every dog owner is a conscientious rule follower as we're sure you are... Another important distinction- Kingston has a town-wide leash law that extends to all conservation areas - see the town website for details.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-VcfLTB":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-kfNVZb","n-nSLPYY"]},"n-OEBchb":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-nCjaej","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-2RXNZh":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-VcfLTB","n-OEBchb"]},"n-UobDwf":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Natural History \u0026amp; Ecology","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Ti8rAV":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Bay Farm is one of few fields remaining in Kingston. Many native perennial plants grow here, including the goldenrod and Joe-Pye-weed shown to the right, and many pollinators reap the rewards, including many species of bees, butterflies, wasps, and even hummingbirds (even day-flying moths that LOOK like hummingbirds!). The flooded forest is a tangle of thickets with native plants like red maple and red cedar, as well as invasive plants like ornamental bittersweet and multi-flora rose. This is a great area for birdwatching due to the variation of habitats; Yellow-breasted Chat occupy the thickets, Song, Swamp, and Savannah Sparrows occupy the fields, and Canada Geese, American Black Ducks, and Buffleheads occupy Kingston Bay. Phenology, or the change of seasons, can easily be embraced and observed as fields are mowed, bay-views open up from dropping leaves, and the first blooms of spring emerge.  ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Y3d6Sv":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-UobDwf","n-Ti8rAV"]},"n-OHluvt":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-HTGAs9","isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-JhwGiK":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-Y3d6Sv","n-OHluvt"]},"n-2dEf1c":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Blackwater Memorial Forest","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-uhdAnK":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"General Information"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-FDHsY8":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 45.66 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Thanks to a generous donation of easement by the Ruffini and Gangi families, the Town offers a gravel parking area for access, located between 25 and 27 Winthrop Street. There is parking for roughly 6 cars, an informational kiosk, and the trail leads north from the parking area.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Historically this was the headwaters of Sampson\u0026apos;s Brook, fed from springs bubbling up in acidic fen habitat. The fen was formerly dominated by Atlantic white cedar trees before they were clear-cut and the fen flooded through installation of an earthen dike and the installation of ditches, creating a four-acre cranberry bog in the 1930s. The location of the parking lot was a pasture associated with a dairy farm. The trees cleared for the installation of the parking area were young post-agricultural regenerating white pines, clearly identified by their multi-trunked habit, caused by white pine weevil damage when growing in open sun. The cranberry bog, owned by Frederick Carlson, ceased production in the early 2000s. Area residents used the existing bog roads and trails for passive recreation. During the COVID-19 pandemic, a developer signed a P+S to purchase the property for residential housing development, triggering the Town\u0026apos;s Right of First Refusal under the Commonwealth\u0026apos;s Chapter 61 agricultural tax break program. Local residents banded together, raising money and gaining support for the purchase of the property. The Town exercised their Right, and the property was purchased using Community Preservation Act funds as well as Local Acquisitions for Natural Diversity (L.A.N.D.) grant funds. The preservation of this excellent natural area was the epitome of grassroots conservation efforts, now available for all to enjoy.\u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Eftmbf":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-2dEf1c","n-uhdAnK","n-FDHsY8"]},"n-s63ie0":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-wH9vM6","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fill","fill":{"x":0.7607361963190185,"y":0.6138125},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-4Je7vy":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-Eftmbf","n-s63ie0"]},"n-qwyZGB":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Recreation"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-wKdjaA":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The same trails and bog roads (2-track dirt/grass paths) mentioned earlier still exist, providing a short loop around the former bog, a longer loop around the entire wetland complex, and side trails through the woods (trails to the north have more strenous topography). The map on the right only estimates the trail locations, but it\u0026apos;s a good approximation. The property is bisected by powerlines that run to the east and west. Over time we hope to connect this property to other area open spaces through trail easements and agreements. As with all of our properties, this one is off-limits to motorized vehicles."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-zTE4b5":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-qwyZGB","n-wKdjaA"]},"n-IUHgNG":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-RVWHmS","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-v2fMue":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-zTE4b5","n-IUHgNG"]},"n-KgMyNK":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-o9dIhS":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Blackwater Memorial Forest consists of roughly 4 acres of retired cranberry bog with associated infrastructure, roughly 8 acres of acidic fen habitat, and roughly 33 acres of mature white pine-oak woodlands. The powerlines provide a permanent early successional habitat component, which adds to the floral and faunal diversity of the site. Sampson\u0026apos;s Brook, which runs south from the site, flows through Blackwater Swamp and its Atlantic white cedar habitats into Blackwater Pond. Stony Brook (or Hall\u0026apos;s Brook depending on the map you\u0026apos;re looking at) connects Blackwater Pond to the Jones River estuary and Kingston Bay. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-hOXS6X":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Over the winter of 2024-25, we\u0026apos;re beginning an ecological restoration project at the site, reestablishing the headwaters of Sampson\u0026apos;s Brook, minimizing stagnant water, and increasing rare habitas and floral diversity. The project is expected to boost biodiversity and the carrying capacity of the property, while providing better stream connectivity for diadromous fish species, in particular the catadromous American eel. Young eels are voracious predators of mosquito larvae, performing a vital human health service free of charge or pesticides. See the video below from Jones River Ecology, and see their YouTube for more great coverage of our area\u0026apos;s ecology. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-i6oui3":{"type":"embed","data":{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GpPpBwZ_s8A","embedType":"video","title":"2020 American Eel Mosquito Control Highlights","description":"Early August 2020, JRWA introduced ten glass eels to a 120 gallon aquarium and began feeding them mosquito larvae to demonstrate their efficacy at controlling mosquito populations.","thumbnailUrl":"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GpPpBwZ_s8A/hqdefault.jpg","providerUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/","height":480,"width":854,"caption":"2020 American Eel Mosquito Control Highlights","isEmbedSupported":true,"display":"inline","embedSrc":"https://cdn.embedly.com/widgets/media.html?a=0\u0026src=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fembed%2FGpPpBwZ_s8A%3Ffeature%3Doembed\u0026display_name=YouTube\u0026url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fwatch%3Fv%3DGpPpBwZ_s8A\u0026image=https%3A%2F%2Fi.ytimg.com%2Fvi%2FGpPpBwZ_s8A%2Fhqdefault.jpg\u0026type=text%2Fhtml\u0026schema=youtube"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-HA1qeC":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-KgMyNK","n-o9dIhS","n-hOXS6X","n-i6oui3"]},"n-eRWJFQ":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-gbeZa4","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-JWjDi8":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-HA1qeC","n-eRWJFQ"]},"n-c1apQv":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":""},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-cFEEpy":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Calista Farm","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-SmBR7U":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"General Information"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-xPwR9x":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize: \u003c/strong\u003e~10 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eA two-vehicle paved parking area off Elder Avenue was installed in summer of 2023, with an informational display soon to follow. Visitors are NOT permitted to cross the train tracks by the salt marsh.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory: \u003c/strong\u003eThis 10-acre property was purchased in 2010 using both Community Preservation Act funds and a generous gift from L. Knife \u0026amp; Sons just down the road. The area was formerly in agricultural use, but the threat of development led the town to purchase the property and designate it as open space. The property has been a hub of human habitation since the ice age receded, between access to the coastal resources (fish, shellfish, salt hay and much more) and Elder\u0026apos;s Spring, a crystal-clear flow of fresh water that bubbled up through white sand at the edge of the salt marsh just northeast of this property (at least it did until someone decided it would make a better \u0026quot;duck pond\u0026quot; in the late 19th century -frown-). See the arrow in the map below for the former location of Elder\u0026apos;s Spring, now on private property - please keep off of private property so we can get along with our neighbors, thanks! See the Conservation page on the town website for full conservation land rules and regs. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-5ujFH4":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-c1apQv","n-cFEEpy","n-SmBR7U","n-xPwR9x"]},"n-X9MeAD":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-Q6KTwn"}},"n-CqnvIk":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-5ujFH4","n-X9MeAD"]},"n-B778jp":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-H8WqOD":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"We have recently started mowing a loop trail in the fields and will continue to do so as funding allows- but keep in mind it's only mowed once per month, so it will not be perfect walking conditions. There are a few short, unmarked trails leading from the field towards the salt marsh, through a coastal woodland. There are great opportunities for botanizing, birding, butterflying, and studying of other insects like bees and beetles. Crossing of the railroad tracks is not permitted and may result in fines or legal action.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-CFFXQV":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-B778jp","n-H8WqOD"]},"n-l8nH6v":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-jZMUtQ","isExpandable":true,"expressImage":"r-bQejyl","expressImageDataId":"r-9p4yPX"},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fill","fill":{"x":0.39805825242718446,"y":0.5698125},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-WR2DO2":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-CFFXQV","n-l8nH6v"]},"n-HpQHQf":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-UyNl2e":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Calista Farm houses a number of native herbaceous meadow plants, including butterfly milkweed (shown to the right), little bluestem, goldenrod, and common milkweed. Butterfly enthusiasts can find monarchs, cabbage whites, and various species of skippers. White-tailed deer, eastern gray squirrels, and coyotes can be seen in the fields. The abutting coastal woodland is secondary growth and late successional, with very few pockets of early successional forest. In recent years we have shifted the mowing regime to be more ecologically proactive, mowing later in the season to allow native plants to produce seed, and limiting mowing to no more than half of the field in a given year in order to maintain populations of stem-nesting invertebrates (many native pollinators nest in stems- if you mow, away they go!). We're also contracting methodical, targeted herbicide treatment of invasive species in order to improve habitat quality and increase biodiversity and the carrying capacity of the site. You may notice black swallow-wort, an invasive vine from Europe that may act as a Monarch butterfly population sink- it's thought that they'll lay their eggs on it (it is a milkweed relative) but the caterpillars can't feed, and quickly die. Another widespread invasive as of this writing is spotted knapweed, another European species with bright purple flowers that seems to bloom all year, which provides great nectar for generalist pollinators, but grows so densely that it outcompetes most native species, causing declines in biodiversity. For more information on invasive species see the MIPAG website (Massachusetts Invasive Plant Advisory Group) or contact the Kingston Conservation Department.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-RUZLAz":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-HpQHQf","n-UyNl2e"]},"n-3hm0aY":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-qpv0tN"}},"n-DmDtuA":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-RUZLAz","n-3hm0aY"]},"n-ZHfJGB":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h2","text":"Camp Nekon"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-uSEgUm":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-OyCxJm":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e ~240 acres as of this writing, expected to grow by 20 acres, as Mass DOT has agreed to sell us their surplus parcel adjacent to Rte 44 (has existing trails and GREAT topography).\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eSouth Kingston, just north of Rte 44. Abuts Smelt Pond to the NE, to the west is Kingston State Forest (DCR) and the Indian Pond Country Club neighborhood. There is a sizable dirt parking area off Monk\u0026apos;s Hill Road, with an informational kiosk, and a small dirt parking area with primitive boat launch at the end of Monk\u0026apos;s Hill Road, where it meets Smelt Pond. There is a small, primitive roadside parking area by the gate off Raboth Road, which can be accessed from Pond View Drive or the mall parking lot. Both Raboth Road and Monk\u0026apos;s Hill Road are rough, dirt roads that may be hazardous at times, especially for vehicles with low ground clearance.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e The residents of Kingston voted to purchase this land (then called Camp Ne-Kon) from the Plymouth Bay Girl Scout Council in 1975, with a near-unanimous vote. It was held as general municipal land until 2014, when the majority of it was transferred to the care of the Conservation Commission, and 2023, when the entirety became conservation land, including the \u0026quot;Offal Pits\u0026quot; on the north side of Raboth Road. Over the years important parcels were added through generous donations by the Wager, Glassford, and Tusing families. Once under the jurisidiction of the Conservation Commission, steps were taken to minimize illegal offroading activity, which had caused significant damage through the decades, not only to the trails, but to the delicate pine barrens ecology, including sedimentation of vernal pools and rare Atlantic Coastal Plain Ponds. As far as colonial and precolonial history, not much is known about this area. In John Gray\u0026apos;s 1831 map of Kingston, the area is detailed as \u0026quot;Barren Pine plain,\u0026quot; a nod to the pine barrens habitat, whose poor soils would make conversion to agriculture relatively fruitless. It\u0026apos;s thought that the famous hermit of 19th century Kingston, Daniel Fuller, lived in the area around Smelt Pond in a tiny cabin. He lived until 1894, supposedly making his living off bounties on crows and woodchucks, or providing fresh ducks for people in town to cook. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-0gzNid":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed","position":"start","size":"small"},"children":["n-ZHfJGB","n-uSEgUm","n-OyCxJm"]},"n-pKYN8s":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-KMUCnV"}},"n-fYgkRD":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-0gzNid","n-pKYN8s"]},"n-jT7RBw":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Pu7tYI":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"THERE ARE NO MOTORIZED VEHICLES ALLOWED (here or at any town conservation property. See the MA DCR website for legal offroading locations)","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-VkhdeK":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Camp Nekon is a great place for walking, hiking, leashed dog walking (Kingston has a town-wide leash law), cycling, birding or other naturalizing. It\u0026apos;s one of the premier hunting areas in town, and Smelt Pond provides some decent fishing action for the kids. While it may not provide many large fish, we hope our efforts to control invasive aquatic plants there will pay dividends over time. To the west is Kingston State Forest, managed by DCR. We\u0026apos;ve worked with them to establish the blue and green long-distance trail loop seen on the map to the right. This trail system is by no means perfect, but passable, and improving every year. Our property management is mostly volunteer-based, so please join our stewardship program if interested in helping out (QR code at beginning and end of this presentation). This trail system also ties in with the extensive trails at the Muddy Pond Wilderness Preserve (\u003ca href=\"https://newildernesstrust.org/projects/muddy-pond-wilderness-preserve/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://newildernesstrust.org/projects/muddy-pond-wilderness-preserve/\u003c/a\u003e), accessible to the south of Camp Nekon by passing through a graffiti-lined \u0026quot;wildlife\u0026quot; tunnel under Route 44. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-3Tf3S5":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-9IOn17","caption":"Ovenbird photo by Lilium Birder"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-RVqEAX":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-HdFVHu","caption":"Eastern Towhee picture by Cameron Johnson"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-fgqe41":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-UJNH02","caption":"Pine warbler photo by Lilium Birder"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-h4uRaF":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Pine Barrens Birds!"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Ls7x3a":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Camp Nekon is a great birdwatching spot, hosting many species through the seasons. Eastern Towhees are ubiquitous here in the summer months. If you hear something flicking through the dry leaves on the forest floor here, it's probably a towhee. Listen for their raspy \"SCREET!\" call that's repeated fequently, or their song, phoonetically described as \"Drink your TEEEEAAAA!\" You can't beat the color pattern either, just a spectacular bird. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-6Q5DWK":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Other avian denizens of Camp Nekon include: ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-akgoF2":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"\u003cli\u003eThe colorful Baltimore Oriole, which are more frequently heard than seen, making woven nests that hang from small branches high in the canopy.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eThe Great Crested Flycatcher, a loud, conspicuous, LARGE member of the \u0026quot;tyrant flycatcher\u0026quot; family, voracious predators of flying insects.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003ePine Warblers can be seen and heard flitting around the trunk and branches of pine trees, gleaning insects as they go.  \u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eOvenbirds sing loudly from the trees in summer, \u0026quot;TEACHER, TEACHER, TEACHER, TEACHER, TEACHER\u0026quot; while looking for a mate. They also forage on the ground as towhees do. Robert Frost dedicated a 1916 poem to these birds, which starts: \u0026quot;\u003cem\u003eThere is a singer everyone has heard, Loud, a mid-summer and a mid-wood bird, Who makes the solid tree trunks sound again\u003c/em\u003e.\u0026quot; Listen for their call in summer, it\u0026apos;s hard to miss! \u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eTons of other species! What have you seen there? Use eBird to log your sightings, it helps inform management tactics- the eBird smartphone app makes reporting easy! \u003c/li\u003e","type":"bullet-list"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-sQDBRG":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-jT7RBw","n-Pu7tYI","n-VkhdeK","n-3Tf3S5","n-RVqEAX","n-fgqe41","n-h4uRaF","n-Ls7x3a","n-6Q5DWK","n-akgoF2"]},"n-UteIHi":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-LYK4hs","expressImage":"r-9We8C5","expressImageDataId":"r-PPbbsA","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-JzH6mM":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-sQDBRG","n-UteIHi"]},"n-YfoKia":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-NyJDol":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Atlantic Coastal Pine Barrens \u0026quot;end\u0026quot; in Kingston, with Camp Nekon being the most northern major parcel of barrens habitat in Kingston. It is not classic barrens habitat, as fire has been suppressed here for generations, which leads to a shift towards dominance of white pine over pitch pine. Pitch pine, like many barrens species, is fire-adapted, needing fire for best seed release from their resinous cones, as well as for a leg up in the constant competition between plants for available resources. Without fire, or similar management, barrens habitats give way to more common habitat types- in this case, it\u0026apos;s white pine-oak woodlands. Still, the area around Camp Nekon maintains enough pine barrens characteristics to perform the important function of supporting the diversity of pine barrens flora and fauna. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Y8Lfgn":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The glacial origins of the landforms here created diverse topography and rare wetland types. Vernal pools dot the landscape here, providing breeding sites for spotted salamanders and wood frogs, among many other amphibian and insect species. These pools also provide much-needed water and food - they are major drivers of the local food chain in the barrens! Other important glacial features here are Atlantic Coastal Plain Ponds, globally rare habitats that often contain rare plant species found in no other habitats on Earth. Rocky Pond, close to the Monk's Hill gate, is one example, but there are many in south Kingston. The best example of an Atlantic Coastal Plain Pondshore community is Muddy Pond, just south of Camp Nekon. The summer blooms of the rare Plymouth gentian and New England boneset are beyond compare. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-NhUXRu":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The Conservation Commission has contracted an update to Camp Nekon's Forest Management Plan, aligning the plan with the Foresters for the Birds program. This plan details the different forest stands on the property and provides suggestions on how to improve the habitat for birds as well as other wildlife, mainly through selective harvests that would allow establishment of more productive heaths and groundcover beneath the canopy, and creation of more protective structure through accumulation of fallen branches and deadfall. Whether such steps are ultimately taken will be up to the Commission, and public comment will be an important step. Either way, we hope you'll get out there and enjoy this amazing property!","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-FxLLCb":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-YfoKia","n-NyJDol","n-Y8Lfgn","n-NhUXRu"]},"n-RqT4dh":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-edG0mf","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-rKwRpB":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-FxLLCb","n-RqT4dh"]},"n-h6adul":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Cranberry Watershed Preserve","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-TOEjP0":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-KwdaWt":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e ~308 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eOne of our most used properties, \u0026quot;CWP\u0026quot; consists of former cranberry bogs, wetlands, and forest, adjacent to the Jones River from Lake Street down to Grove Street where it meets Three Rivers Basin. Due to the recent Albertini annex, there is access with parking in three separate locations- On Grove Street (look for the kiosk), on Lake Street across from the Forge Pond Dam, and behind the Silver Lake Regional Middle School near the water tower and athletic fields. Much of the area is in a state of regrowth after former agricultural use. The site would be a great candidate for ecological restoration, particularly to restore Pine Brook, a Jones River tributary. Still, this property holds great opportunities to view wildlife in all seasons.\u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-jcaojs":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-h6adul","n-TOEjP0","n-KwdaWt"]},"n-fLXgJo":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-EQ0uMh"}},"n-TZdpcH":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-jcaojs","n-fLXgJo"]},"n-lLL46d":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"History: ","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Dr4Qmi":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"These properties have been in cranberry agriculture for a long time. Some of the bogs, particularly on the east side of the property, were created in the 1990s, but many of the bogs were in existence prior to 1960, thought to be mainly built between 1940 and 1945, but a few may possibly date back to the 19th century. Bogs were typically created by modifying wetlands, but occasionally bogs were dug from uplands. Often streams were dammed in  order to retain water for flooding and irrigation, as was the case here for Pine Brook, a tributary of the Jones River, and other small streams. Removal of obsolete dams for the purpose of stream continuity has become the new standard, backed by countless scientific studies. Over the long term, our hope is to restore the streams and wetlands at this property, bolstering diadromous fish and countess other species. Note: \u0026quot;\u003cem\u003eDiadromous\u003c/em\u003e fish\u0026quot; are species that either live in the ocean and breed in freshwater habitats (\u003cem\u003eanadromous\u003c/em\u003e species, like river herring, white perch, rainbow smelt, etc.) and species that live in freshwater and migrate to the ocean to breed (\u003cem\u003ecatadromous\u003c/em\u003e species, the only one here being the American eel, \u003cem\u003eAnguilla rostrata\u003c/em\u003e- an AMAZING species worth your attention, and a voracious predator of mosquito larvae when young). ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-vv7Tqc":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"One ten-acre inset parcel not owned by the town belongs to A. W. Perry Co., who may have used this parcel as a shingle-cutting lot, though the company mainly shifted from shoe manufacturing to real estate in the late 19th century. It currently sits untouched, in a forested condition, adding to the available habitat in the area. The parcel is landlocked by parcels owned by the Town of Kingston and protected by Article 97 of the MA Constitution. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-s2cWFN":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The Town of Kingston purchased the first parcels in 1998 with help from the Commonwealth, and the Albertini property was added in 2015, also assisted by state grant funds.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-aU0D4u":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-njpppV","caption":"Example of a 19th century bog harvest in Kingston. This is thought to be the Keith \u0026 Adams bog harvest in October of 1893 - photo courtesy of the Kingston Public Library collections. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-o39Zty":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-lLL46d","n-Dr4Qmi","n-vv7Tqc","n-s2cWFN","n-aU0D4u"]},"n-OpFDFn":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-a8wzCO"}},"n-162fgk":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-o39Zty","n-OpFDFn"]},"n-WRRYMV":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-dpctUN":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-cewrFU":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"NOTE: The color-coded trails seen in the map to the right have not been marked in the field as of February 2024. The trails exist, as do many unwanted \"social\" trails, but color-blazing is upcoming, stay tuned. The map is available for download from the Conservation page on the town website- using an app like AvenzaMaps you can see your location on the map using a smartphone's positioning system.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-2JbZg7":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Cranberry Watershed Preserve is one of a few town conservation areas where you can hike portions of the Bay Circuit Trail, a 230-mile trail running from Kingston to Newburyport, roughly following the I-95/495 corridor (see baycircuit.org for maps and info). The BCT is shown in the map to the right as a yellow/black line, and it's marked in the field with white BCT placards and white paint blazes.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-KUWBjB":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-HP7qV5","caption":"From baycircuit.org - overview of the 230-mile Bay Circuit Trail, which starts (or ends) at Bay Farm, by the Kingston/Duxbury town line."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-sqRZZ2":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-8YK2PJ"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-80s0k1":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Other recreational opportunities include hunting and fishing - I haven't heard anything on the quality of fishing at Howard Brook Reservoir and Pine Brook Reservoir, but they may provide good opportunities to get kids interested in fishing, as they have ample shorefishing access, and would likely be productive with bobber and worm. Birdwatching and naturalizing would be productive here, with the extensive wetland habitats. It's a great site to see different species of dragonfly and damselfly, and if you're lucky (or quiet and patient), you may see mink, otter, and several other species of weasel. Try using eBird (website or smartphone app) to record your bird sightings, and iNaturalist to record all other taxa. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ejIILo":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Note: Silver Lake Regional School District maintains cross-country running trails in the woods at Cranberry Watershed Preserve. ALL DOGS MUST BE LEASHED per Kingston's town-wide leash law, and we will issue fines for noncompliance, particularly as prior issues on this property have resulted in student injury. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-m0OqIa":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-WRRYMV","n-dpctUN","n-cewrFU","n-2JbZg7","n-KUWBjB","n-sqRZZ2","n-80s0k1","n-ejIILo"]},"n-UmobHZ":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-8Vuf0f","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-ZUyFLc":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-m0OqIa","n-UmobHZ"]},"n-2zHp1A":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-siU8fC":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The jewel of Cranberry Watershed Preserve, and the main driving force behind its preservation, is the Jones River, a 7.5-mile river that runs from Silver Lake to Kingston Bay. The Jones and its estuary have provided bountiful food in the form of fish and shellfish for thousands of years, from the end of the last ice age until 19th century dams and other human influences caused populations of diadromous fish to crash. Restoration of these fish runs is an important goal of our program, in conjunction with local nonprofits like the Jones River Watershed Association. Cranberry agriculture often has a heavy hand when it comes to local wetlands, particularly when creating bogs. Here, not only were the banks of the Jones River modified, but two major tributaries have been straightened, channelized, and dammed, in Pine Brook and Howard Brook. Ecological restoration of these streams and the associated wetlands and cranberry bogs is a major program goal for Kingston Conservation over the long term. These properties contain many remnant culverts, flumes, dams and other features that artificially divert or contain water, and have the undesirable effect of blocking fish passage, raising water temperature, reducing water quality, and minimizing biodiversity. Stay tuned for restoration proposals in upcoming years!","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-S9NVpE":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"There are several vernal pools on the property, providing habitat for spring-breeding vernal pool obligate species like spotted salamanders, wood frogs and fairy shrimp, and species that make use of vernal pools, like gray treefrogs, spring peepers, American and Fowler\u0026apos;s toads. Importantly, the preserved forest around these pools allows these species the upland space they need to survive, especially over the winter. Many scientific studies show that the development setbacks commonly used in wetlands permitting are woefully insufficient for maintaining long-term viable populations of vernal pool amphibians. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-xj1ALA":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-Rypnpp","caption":"A spotted salamander found in an upland hideout under a small log, with an isopod and white invertebrate eggs. This species needs vernal pools in order to to reproduce successfully, but also requires large protected upland areas around the pools for overwintering, as well as feeding and avoiding dessication when the pools dry up and the weather is hot and dry. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-roVS9Q":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Beyond the wetlands, Cranberry Watershed Preserve consists mainly of white pine-oak woodlands, a common regional forest type, but one capable of supporting many species. The research of renowned author-naturalist Doug Tallamy shows that white oak alone can support over 400 species of Lepidopteran (butterfly \u0026 moth) larvae alone, and eastern white pine can support more than 250. Keep in mind that's only one taxonomic group, nevermind the diversity of beetles, bees and wasps, true bugs, etc. - more attention is paid to Lepidopteran larvae (caterpillars) because our local and migratory songbirds feed their young almost exclusively on them, and they need LOTS of them in order to survive. Most of the pine-oak forest here is between 70 and 90 years old, with some areas having filled in since the agricultural use ceased. Some of the cranberry bogs have converted to forest, a combination of red maple swamp and mixed pine habitats depending on the hydrology. One of the goals of habitat restoration would be to ensure that no \"artificial\" forest types remained- i.e. a pine forest growing in sand on top of a former bog- most of those areas ideally would be converted back to their original wetland soils and hydrology. There was a Forest Stewardship Plan prepared for the property, but it is out of date.  "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-PgDOoK":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-2zHp1A","n-siU8fC","n-S9NVpE","n-xj1ALA","n-roVS9Q"]},"n-PMaNXe":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-sHtOh6"}},"n-WYhaFH":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-PgDOoK","n-PMaNXe"]},"n-A3QrCR":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"F. Sherburne Carter Jr. Preserve","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-9XKJZK":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Zr03bR":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize: \u003c/strong\u003e18 acres (51 acres when added to the 33 acres provided by the adjacent Arbor Hills Pedestrian \u0026amp; Conservation Easement and the Macfarlane Farms RDEOS Open Space).\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eAccessed from a dirt parking area on the south side of Rte 106 across from 143 Wapping Road.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e This 18-acre parcel was donated by Arbor Hills, LLC during the development of Arbor Hills Estates, through the town\u0026apos;s approval of an RDEOS plan (Residential Development Encouraging Open Space) in the mid-2000s. The cranberry bogs here were owned and managed by Lyon and F. Sherburne Carter, Jr. as co-owners of Carter Cranberry. Now the property is under the care and custody of the Kingston Conservation Commission. The adjacent Arbor Hills open space property is privately held, but a Pedestrian \u0026amp; Conservation Easement allows passive recreation on existing trails. The Macfarlane Farms RDEOS Open Space was recently added to the Town\u0026apos;s conservation land as well, and connects to Carter Preserve through the Arbor Hills Easement. Other nearby properties are private open space, with no public access, intended only as wildlife habitat and as buffers to development and infrastructure issues. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-g21vCo":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-A3QrCR","n-9XKJZK","n-Zr03bR"]},"n-eYMsUU":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-hCDAb9"}},"n-NPtbHN":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-g21vCo","n-eYMsUU"]},"n-5cHQo1":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-okEQNw":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Trails at \"Carter Preserve\" connect to a trail passing around the Arbor Hills development and a trail at Macfarlane Farms, as part of the \"Cross-town Trail.\" Well-established paths exist in the form of the old bog roads- relatively flat and easy to traverse, though the site looks quite different post-agriculture. Much of the former agricultural bogs now contain a mix of wetland forest and riparian upland shrub thickets and forest. This makes Carter Preserve a great place to take a short hike when getting out of the sun is important in the summer months. The trails traverse several habitat types and through a diversity of tree species rivalled nowhere else in town. The former bog reservoir is a good place to see turtles and aquatic invertebrates (lots of whirligig beetles, dragonflies and damselflies), among other species. If lucky, you may catch a glimpse at a mink or otter, maybe a fox. This is a great site for birdwatching, particularly during the spring and fall migrations, as the wetland forests are important resting and feeding areas for birds travelling through the area. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-AkVKat":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Hunting is not allowed at this property due to proximity to local residences. Please observe the town-wide leash law and be sure to pick up after your pets. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-jPBv5h":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-5cHQo1","n-okEQNw","n-AkVKat"]},"n-HKlulR":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-L9u1IS","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-Nbgy18":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-jPBv5h","n-HKlulR"]},"n-rlpYxY":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-RvhND0":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The habitat at F. Sherburne Carter Jr. Preserve is different from many of the other conservation properties in town. While most of them are dominated by white pine-oak forest, or pitch pine-oak forest in south Kingston, here you have a much more diverse set of trees, likely from the mix of wetland types found here, and the agricultural history of the site. Unfortunately, as is true of many local conservation properties where cranberry bogs once functioned, the bogs have been left to go fallow, and forest is forming on top of the bog soils. This forest is not a natural habitat, as the soils are modified for agriculture, sort of upland soils just above standing water, often with a high sand content. As these forests grow, the dense canopy prevents any understory vegetation from forming, limiting biodiversity, and when added to the soil structure, often results in a high rate of blowovers during storm events. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-jUtCgz":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"One way to deal with these issues, and boost biodiversity and carrying capacity, is through ecological restoration. Carter Preserve is ripe for this sort of work, as there is an artificial pond on-site through the damming of a small tributary of the Jones River, a series of water control structures and ditches around the former bogs, and this network of dense young forest growing on the old bog pads. From a site visit with DER (MA Division of Ecological Restoration), it's thought that the best site condition for boosting biodiversity and carrying capacity would be through removal of water control structures in order to reestablish stream connectivity for diadromous fish, including American eel, the voracious mosquito predator, filling the obsolete bog ditches (also helps control mosquito populations), and establishing sandplain grassland habitat on the old bog pads on either side of the stream. This plan would likely leave pockets of the most diverse forested sections, focusing efforts on the bog closest to Wapping Road. Sandplain grassland is one of the rarest habitat types in the region, supporting many rare species, and needing occasional disturbance to avoid the successional process (fire, mowing, other natural disasters, etc.). This restoration work will need extensive planning, grant funding, and permitting by local, state and federal agencies. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-3F1Hgi":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-rlpYxY","n-RvhND0","n-jUtCgz"]},"n-lB96GP":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-ewo1mu","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.6167355371900827,"y":0.2018125},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-v964AE":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-3F1Hgi","n-lB96GP"]},"n-xemHyT":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Hall Conservation Area","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ZWE2Es":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-86bcua":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 45 acres, including the 3.7 acre Blackwater Swamp parcel across Stony Brook.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Easily accessed from the large gravel parking area at the Opachinski Sports Complex, a series of town recreational fields. From the westernmost ballfield, walk around the gate and along the 2-track dirt road betwen the field and the MBTA tracks. The main trail leads into the woods from there.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e The Town of Kingston purchased this land from the Hall family in 2010, for the purposes of open space/conservation as well as adding athletic fields to the Opachinski complex. Ultimately, fields could not be constructed due to significant cultural and historic aspects of the site, and the entire site is now protected by a conservation restriction. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-kWzM4I":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-xemHyT","n-ZWE2Es","n-86bcua"]},"n-sBj5dC":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-QtW7hH","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true,"attribution":"Original artwork by Elizabeth James Perry, Aquinnah Tribal Consultant and traditional artist \u003ca href=\"https://elizabethjamesperry.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://elizabethjamesperry.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://elizabethjamesperry.com/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/a\u003e"},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-XI9NSv":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-kWzM4I","n-sBj5dC"]},"n-4VdpaI":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ugpGqQ":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Existing trails can be seen on the map to the right; they lead from an open area by the SW corner of the furthermost athletic field at the Opachinski Sports Complex off Pottle Street. The trails are not long, but lead through a variety of habitat types and offer great opportunities for viewing wildlife, particularly birdwatching. From the northern trail you can view mature Atlantic white cedar trees, as the trail leads onto a peninsula within the rare Atlantic White Cedar Swamp (AWCS) habitat. As of this writing, hunting is not allowed on this property. Most of the property consists of wetlands, primarily AWCS. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-DQ4aU6":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-4VdpaI","n-ugpGqQ"]},"n-6V5Yvq":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-C0peqx","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-B4vrRz":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-DQ4aU6","n-6V5Yvq"]},"n-ZCYqS9":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026 Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-1svW1p":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Uplands here consist of the typical white pine-oak forest of the area, while the area initially cut in preparation for the playing fields is now regenerating forest, which provides a whole different type of habitat and structure, as shrubs, forbs, and stump sprouts give way to fast-growing tree species. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-P0nY7r":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The wetlands are the most important habitat here, mainly the Atlantic White Cedar Swamp (AWCS). This is a habitat not only rare regionally, but worldwide, as AWC habitats have declined over the last few centuries for various reasons, mostly caused by human actions. In Kingston, as in many areas, these habitats were considered worthless, once the trees had been cut. The first playground in town was constructed by residents through filling an AWC bog that had been cut over. These were the days before wetlands regulations, before we knew how valuable these habitats are. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Ljtsqz":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-ws4TlR","caption":"Courtesy Kingston Public Library collections- construction of Captain Bailey Playground ca. 1926 where Atlantic white cedar had been fully harvested from a natural bog. Copied from \"Civic Progress of Kingston\" by Sarah Y. Bailey."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-J1eBTg":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Speaking of the trees, AWC was (and still is) considered one of the most useful, as its natural insect and rot-resistant qualities make it a fine lumber for posts and shingles. Throughout the region they were clearcut, and the species\u0026apos; slow growth means a long interval before they could be cut again. They are also sensitive to changes in hydrology, and many local AWC swamps have converted to red maple swamps through human modifications to local hydrology, such as installation of railroad beds, dams and other water control structures.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-oOQwbj":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-ZpIvFE","caption":"Charles Drew, father of Emily Drew Fuller, using the rail and walkway system he built for his post-retirement enterprise. From his house on Summer Street (just east of Hall C.A.), he harvested AWC for sale. Small trees would be used to make fence posts, large ones would be split into shingles, likely using a hand tool known as a froe. Photo courtesy Kingston Public Library collections."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-68WN14":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Atlantic white cedar habitats support a diversity of species, including rare species such as Hessel's Hairstreak, a beautiful green butterfly whose larvae (caterpillars) can only feed on the foliage of AWC. The adults may be seen through May and early June, often found in AWC swamps nectaring on flowers of highbush blueberry (Vaccinium corymbosum) or chokeberries (Aronia spp.) where gaps in the canopy exist, though they may be found nearby nectaring on other flowers. Among other threats, the main one being habitat loss or alteration, the use of aerial insecticides is thought to be a potential driver of this species' decline. If you see any around, be sure to let us know, and take pictures!"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-mqGjol":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-5LqrMJ","caption":"Hessel's Hairstreak (Callophrys hesseli), photo courtesy MA Natural Heritage \u0026 Endangered Species Program, taken by M.W. Nelson"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ZlZxro":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-ZCYqS9","n-1svW1p","n-P0nY7r","n-Ljtsqz","n-J1eBTg","n-oOQwbj","n-68WN14","n-mqGjol"]},"n-PRYGwx":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-XIHTrk","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5588842975206612,"y":0.7298125},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-KNbmtB":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-ZlZxro","n-PRYGwx"]},"n-f5VEOF":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Hathaway Preserve \u0026amp; Three Rivers Basin","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-i1DKKJ":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-rXGJi7":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 99.8 acres (77.4 acres for Hathaway and 22.4 for Three Rivers Basin)\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eThe main access is off Wapping Road where there is a parking area down a forested 2-track road, just past the powerlines. There is no parking near the former Correira bogs, our access easement is only 10’ wide. The property may also be accessed off Foxworth Lane, by traveling south on the powerlines, following the Bay Circuit Trail through the woods to the Jones River, where a new bridge will cross you into Hathaway Preserve. There is currently no parking or public access for Three Rivers Preserve.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory: \u003c/strong\u003eThe 77.4 acres of land at Hathaway Preserve was purchased in 1996 from Isaac Hathaway, preserved as intact forest in order to protect the Jones River.   \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-1JKnGT":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-AVkecU","caption":"Alternate access off Foxworth Lane (roadside parking only)"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ftjKq4":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The property and river rights were granted to Steven Bradford (grandson of Governor Bradford) circa 1760.  It was called Steven Bradford's Privilege.  The last sawmill working there was around 1900. Steven Bradford and his successors made redware pottery in a building just to the right of the entrance by Roger Correira’s bog.  In order to build one of his bogs in the early 1990’s, Mr. Correira tore down a late 18th century house with outbuildings where the redware was formed.  He tried to give away the house prior to razing it and had someone from Plimoth Patuxet come look at the site before the building was torn down.  The kiln foundation was probably nearby. There are a lot of redware pottery shards on the access road by the bog and a lot of shards in the crossing by Spring Brook.  The Open Space Committee found a multitude of the shards when they dug holes and put in the footings for the boardwalk that was built over the brook in the late 1990’s. Mary Hathaway, Isaac Hathaway’s sister, used to ride her bicycle to the property during the 1930's to dig clay.  She would form big balls of it, wrap them up, and then ride all the way to Plymouth Pottery in the center of Plymouth and sell it to the women who made the Plymouth Pottery. One of the pits where clay was dug is now the vernal pool on the left after you pass the parking area.  The clay is grey in color, but when it is fired in a kiln it turns red from the iron found in the clay. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ZHbQwf":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The Conservation Commission purchased the Three Rivers Basin property from a developer in February of 2003, utilizing state Self-Help Program funds. It consists almost entirely of retired cranberry bogs, with a few small upland areas. Pine Brook, an important tributary of the Jones River, flows through these bogs. There was once a retreat parking area on the north side of Grove Street, but that has since been gated, as passive recreation opportunities from that location were quite limited due to the railroad tracks. There are existing trails on the south side, but they are not maintained. Once a bridge is installed where the white trail at Hathaway Preserve meets the Jones River, the trail can be reopened, and would get a portion of the Bay Circuit Trail off-road.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ALbJ9z":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-f5VEOF","n-i1DKKJ","n-rXGJi7","n-1JKnGT","n-ftjKq4","n-ZHbQwf"]},"n-lKbGQy":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-YLFYzb"}},"n-rulkJd":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-ALbJ9z","n-lKbGQy"]},"n-q7B9be":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-v3Oyy3":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"There are hiking trails marked in white, red, and blue at Hathaway Preserve, as well a section of the Bay Circuit Trail (BCT). There are opportunities for loop trails from the parking area, utilizing either the red trail or BCT, and an out-and-back section of the white trail to the northwest which dead-ends at the Jones River. A groomed, wide, stone dust road follows the powerline cut- a stable walking surface but there is some topography to tend with (the red trail also has some steep elevation gains). Most of the trails are well-marked and stable, though the closer to the river you get, the quicker the trails are encroached by vegetation. There are some great stands of mature white pine-oak forest, and forested wetlands with extensive trails, several boardwalks and bridges.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-UL6Mao":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Some of these bridges and boardwalks are in need of repair, but a replacement bridge over the Jones River was installed in 2022 by the capable AMC trail crew seen to the right. This bridge connects the BCT across the Jones River from Foxworth Lane. Unfortunately, the section of BCT to the north follows roadways until entering Cranberry Watershed Preserve north of Grove Street. One of our long-term goals is to install a bridge at the end of the white trail, with a series of boardwalks through the old bogs at Three Rivers Basin, minimizing a major on-street portion of the BCT.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-PyrXfj":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-q7B9be","n-v3Oyy3","n-UL6Mao"]},"n-1eHy8B":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-eiDYtG"}},"n-B8Wyqn":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-PyrXfj","n-1eHy8B"]},"n-jIMlyC":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The map seen to the right can be downloaded from the Conservation page on the town website. Using a smartphone app like Avenza Maps, you can see your location on the map as you walk. Please be sure to respect private property boundaries, and see our website for full conservation land rules and regulations. Also, please use our volunteer stewardship page to let us know of any issues you come across on the trails!"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-C7raWc":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ZsdcTw":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-jIMlyC","n-C7raWc"]},"n-pFkFET":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-U6xdVt","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-PF8r2d":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-ZsdcTw","n-pFkFET"]},"n-PHAhW5":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026 Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-aSPEy3":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"While the upland habitat at Hathaway Preserve is your typical white pine-oak forest, the Jones River and its associated wetlands provide a wealth of plant diversity, which in turn feeds a diverse set of fauna. In summer months, look for ebony jewelwings, brightly colored damselflies seen to the right, or one of the many other Odonate species in the area (Odonata is the order of predatory flying insects including dragonflies and damselflies). ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-7wDc00":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The property has several bridges that cross the Jones and bordering wetlands","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-fs4ZXv":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The retired bogs and channelized waterways of Three Rivers Basin have great potential for ecological restoration, from removal of unnecessary water control structures, to elimination of ditches and encouragement of native wetland shrubs and forbs. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-oWKSll":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-PHAhW5","n-aSPEy3","n-7wDc00","n-fs4ZXv"]},"n-Sw6yve":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-8Rq3y8","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5295658682634731,"y":0.6738125},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-t9flpq":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-oWKSll","n-Sw6yve"]},"n-gw77aJ":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Marshall Joyce Wildlife Sanctuary","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-TIVzGE":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"General Information"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-NuGGGG":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 1.25 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access: \u003c/strong\u003eLocated at 7 River Street. There is no parking available here, but the site is only a 500-foot walk from the gravel parking area at Mulliken\u0026apos;s Landing.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Purchased in November 2008 for the purpose of preserving open space, utilizing Community Preservation Act funds. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ThgHvL":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-gw77aJ","n-TIVzGE","n-NuGGGG"]},"n-qzwe80":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-UL12kK"}},"n-TVdxy1":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-ThgHvL","n-qzwe80"]},"n-6n5TJf":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Recreation"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-pkEXXv":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Marshall Joyce Sanctuary is managed more for preservation of open space and wildlife habitat, and less for passive recreation. As a small property (just over an acre), recreation potential is limited anyway. Preservation of this land helps protect the Jones River from further encroachment, and provides shelter for wildlife in an increasingly divided landscape (and makes fresh air for you, recharges groundwater, reduces the need for municipal services, the list goes on...). Still, there is a primitive footpath leading from River Street to the salt marsh adjacent to the Jones. Especially in the warmer months, you\u0026apos;ll have a better view from Mulliken\u0026apos;s, but may have this spot to yourself. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-rsTMko":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-6n5TJf","n-pkEXXv"]},"n-VI4Msx":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-ORhKJ1"}},"n-chx1lK":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-rsTMko","n-VI4Msx"]},"n-B4lF0Q":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-FjBphv":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Though small, as described above, this property provides essential services to not only wildlife, but to human health and well-being. The intact forest in the upland portion, though affected by invasive plant species, provides shelter and forage to wildlife. During bird migration seasons you may find numerous species hurriedly feeding here, building energy for continued long-distance flight. The large eastern red cedar trees (really they\u0026apos;re junipers, scientific name \u003cem\u003eJuniperus virginianus\u003c/em\u003e) along the edge of the salt marsh provide a much-desired food source in their \u0026quot;berries,\u0026quot; which are really cones, but wrapped in waxy blue-green flesh. These cones provide meaningful sustenance for many species of birds during the crucial fall-winter months, including Cedar Waxwings, which get their name from an affinity for these waxy \u0026quot;fruits.\u0026quot; This species is host to many species of moths and butterflies, including the exceptionally striking juniper hairstreak. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-L8vp9U":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-FvTyy8","caption":"Adult juniper hairstreak (\u003cem\u003eCallophrys gryneus\u003c/em\u003e) on eastern red cedar, photographed by Dan Davison, sourced from BugGuide.com"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-qdXLiB":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-B4lF0Q","n-FjBphv","n-L8vp9U"]},"n-i9d5pG":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-A9GaKq","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-QDcEyj":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-qdXLiB","n-i9d5pG"]},"n-p68dp8":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Mulliken's Landing","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-egxUT5":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"General Information"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-FoV565":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 2.36 acres (1.06 acres owned by Town of Kingston, 1.30 owned by MA DCR but managed by Kingston Conservation). \u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Located at 1 River Street (at the intersection with Landing Road). Gravel parking area for ~ 5 vehicles. Provides seasonal access to the Jones River for kayaks and canoes.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Purchased in December 2001 utilizing MA DCS Self-Help Program funds. Named after Bob Mulliken, who served on the Conservation Commission from 1963 until 1978, and on the Open Space Committee from 1985 until his passing in November 2001. Bob was responsible for the Town\u0026apos;s first Self-Help Program acquisition, Patuxet Park. As Bob was forced to use a walker later in life, he remained  determined to enjoy the outdoor spaces he loved, and focused on improving accessibility in our open spaces. The completion of the accessible pier project in 2022 was a long time coming, and wouldn\u0026apos;t have been possible had it not been for all of Bob\u0026apos;s work over the years.    \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-irmCgt":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-iLhQss","caption":"Bob Mulliken enjoying the outdoors. Date and source unk. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-KFVWQQ":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-p68dp8","n-egxUT5","n-FoV565","n-irmCgt"]},"n-uYGexY":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-p6ytBW","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-2f1XKr":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-KFVWQQ","n-uYGexY"]},"n-3uIyaS":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Recreation"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ATPRO8":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Aimed at enhancing public access to the Jones River, an accessible pier, ramp and float was installed in 2022 thanks to many volunteers, the Waterfront Committee, the Harbormaster and his staff, the MassTrails program, Community Preservation Act, and the Elizabeth B. Sampson Memorial Fund. The pier is open year-round, but the ramp and float are seasonal- typically installed between April and May, and removed at the end of October. The ramp and float are strongly influenced by tide- at low tide the aluminum ramp is quite steep, at high tide it\u0026apos;s closer to level. While most of the trail/pier was designed to be ADA-compliant, use of the ramp and float may require specialized equipment or assistance, particularly at lower tides. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-uG4FlG":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-FWgloV":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-3uIyaS","n-ATPRO8","n-uG4FlG"]},"n-TPcmEQ":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-9Zqn16","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-Nbsyvg":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-FWgloV","n-TPcmEQ"]},"n-X8GfVT":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Even in the colder months without the ramp and float, the pier provides excellent views of the Jones River winding through salt marsh. It would be a completely peaceful spot to relax were it not for the hum of Route 3 just overhead. Still, it provides great visual appeal, and a great spot to watch for birds and other wildlife. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-pyPpuV":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-SUGWbf":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-X8GfVT","n-pyPpuV"]},"n-FTUYyd":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-a2a1we","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true,"attribution":"Photo by Jimmy Powell, Ecology Program Director for Jones River Watershed Association"},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-1bZI9M":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-SUGWbf","n-FTUYyd"]},"n-YgN45n":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Ecology \u0026amp; Natural History"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-o2Te7k":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Like so many other Kingston Conservation properties, Mulliken\u0026apos;s Landing is located on the Jones River. Extensive salt marsh can be seen from the pier, and looking back towards the uplands you can see the different groups of salt marsh grasses growing in large swaths. There are still a few areas where the invasive \u003cem\u003ePhragmites australis\u003c/em\u003e or common reed has not dominated. You can see many different things here as the seasons change. From spring through fall there is extensive fish activity in this part of the river, within the tide\u0026apos;s reach. Shorebirds like Great Blue Herons and Great Egrets regularly stalk fish along the edge of the river. Red-winged Blackbirds fill the marsh with sound and color through the warm months before heading to wintering grounds across the southern states. Early morning viewers may glimpse river otters, muskrats, or mink if they\u0026apos;re lucky and patient. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-nzdo8K":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"There are many invasive plants growing at this site. Please contact the Conservation Department if you\u0026apos;d like to assist efforts to keep them at bay. In recent years volunteers have been able to cut enough wisteria and Asiatic bittersweet out of the mature basswood trees along the trail that they can finally see the sun! These vines strangle trees, affecting their ability to transport water and sugars, block the leaves from photosynthesizing the sun\u0026apos;s rays, add significant weight, and catch more wind, increasing blowdowns.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-XZyYml":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-YgN45n","n-o2Te7k","n-nzdo8K"]},"n-YZXkyv":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-sqBopO","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-VdiEvv":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-XZyYml","n-YZXkyv"]},"n-ZNtC4P":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Patuxet Park","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-i8Wwmz":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-j5aBhX":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 23.8 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Trailhead is at the end of Basler\u0026apos;s Lane, a cul-de-sac leading off Main Street. There is paved parking for several cars at the end of the paved street, though no spaces are outlined.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Purchased in 1970 utilizing state Self-Help Program funds. Former Commissioner Bob Mulliken was instrumental in preserving this former dairy farm (see Mulliken\u0026apos;s Landing for more info on Bob\u0026apos;s tireless work towards the betterment of Kingston). This area was supposedly used as a summer encampment by ancestors of the Herring Pond Wampanoag Tribe, called the Patuxet in many historical records. \u003ca href=\"https://www.herringpondtribe.org/our-history/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.herringpondtribe.org/our-history/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-UasL1i":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-ZNtC4P","n-i8Wwmz","n-j5aBhX"]},"n-scyBlj":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-7icB09","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-V04KfX":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-UasL1i","n-scyBlj"]},"n-14pUry":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-3Jansv":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"For starters, beware of poison ivy! Particularly in the field behind Smelt Brook, there is poison ivy all over the ground- leaves of three, leave it be is the old adage, and while not super accurate, it works! There is also poison sumac adjacent to the trail just before the brook- NOTE- this is not the sumac with red fuzzy berries you\u0026apos;ll see along the beginning of the trail, that\u0026apos;s staghorn sumac, a harmless beneficial native plant (though some people do report sensitivity to it). Poison sumac has white berries like poison ivy, and generally only grows in wetlands."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-CStaom":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-nbtokJ","caption":"Staghorn sumac is a beneficial native shrub that is often confused with poison sumac. The two look little alike, and are typically found in very different habitats. Poison sumac, like poison ivy, can produce a severe rash when the oils it produces contact our skin. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Zxp3Im":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"There is one main trail leading from Basler\u0026apos;s Lane past Smelt Brook, skirting the first field, leading through woodlands along the brook until reaching the large second field furthest to the west. We\u0026apos;ll be working on connecting the Leland and Scofield Road areas to this trail, connecting that neighborhood not only to our trails, but making shaded pedestrian access to Gray\u0026apos;s Beach possible. There are several short side trails, though some lead to private property. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-HyEJVg":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-97fJY4","caption":"Older map,but relatively accurate. The trail is not maintained in the first field, but skirts it to the south (field is accessed by a spur trail, but be alert for poison ivy)."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-x0HtqX":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-14pUry","n-3Jansv","n-CStaom","n-Zxp3Im","n-HyEJVg"]},"n-e1QQdA":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-JzJE18"}},"n-he74Dt":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-x0HtqX","n-e1QQdA"]},"n-vCmBYZ":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026 Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-3ssRu1":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Patuxet Park is in the long, slow process of recovering from prior use, in this case as a dairy farm. You can see the concrete foundation of a barn on the right as you head down the trail from the parking area. As is typical in these cases, the property is heavily affected by invasive plant species, such as garlic mustard, autumn olive, shrub honeysuckle, burning bush, glossy buckthorn, and Asiatic bittersweet, though there are many others. There are two open field areas slowly reclaimed by shrubs- former pastures losing out to successional processes. The Conservation Department would like to manage these areas as field habitats, as they support many species and improve biodiversity, but the areas are difficult for town equipment to access. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-9N1y5y":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"It is sensible that this area would have been historically used as a summer encampment by the local Wampanoag Tribe, as Smelt Pond bends through here on it\u0026apos;s way from Smelt Pond to Kingston Bay. Though now dammed in several places, with diminished water quality due to inputs from road salt, lawn fertilizer, and dog waste, in precolonial times water in the brook would have been clean, and diadromous fish plenty, from rainbow smelt to river herring and American eel, and possibly a few other species long-since eradicated. Still, when standing on the wooden bridge and looking upstream or down, you could be convinced of a former time. The only invasive plant you\u0026apos;ll see here is a sparse smattering of purple loosestrife, which thankfully has not dominated the wetland habitats along the brook.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-6z5LwB":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-vCmBYZ","n-3ssRu1","n-9N1y5y"]},"n-9IRWk9":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-cruTti"}},"n-bxWFVO":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-6z5LwB","n-9IRWk9"]},"n-EphPrI":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Sampson Park \u0026amp; Faunce Memorial Forest","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-a0gobD":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-d5kmWC":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 170 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Main access is from a dirt/gravel parking area off Elm Street, across from the Jones River Trading Post, with room for roughly 10 vehicles. The Bay Circuit Trail leads out to the Water Department property at 179 South Street, though there is no  parking available there for trail use. \u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Preservation of this area began in 1950 with the 52-acre Walter H. Faunce Memorial Grant, and expanded in the 1960s through additional gifts and purchases. In 1963 the Conservation Commission purchased nearly 100 acres of land, establishing the Elizabeth B. Sampson Memorial Park. A portion of these parcels is also preserved as a water supply protection zone, in addition to the conservation protections. Signs of former agricultural use exist on the property, mainly in the form of old rock walls, and a portion of the property by Furnace Brook was used as a trout hatchery at one point as well.   \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-leRe0p":{"type":"embed","data":{"url":"https://kplma.org/pdfs/people/Walter%20Faunce.pdf","embedType":"pdf","providerUrl":"https://kplma.org","height":780,"width":600,"useSmartEmbed":false,"isEmbedSupported":false,"display":"card","embedSrc":"https://kplma.org/pdfs/people/Walter%20Faunce.pdf","alt":"","hasEmbedOpenButton":true,"isLiveOnMobile":false,"title":"KPL bio- Walter H. Faunce","description":"A one-page biography on Walter H. Faunce, put together by Kingston Public Library archival staff. ","thumbnailResourceId":"r-WR0Qil"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-fH0aPL":{"type":"embed","data":{"url":"https://kplma.org/pdfs/people/Elizabeth%20Sampson.pdf","embedType":"pdf","providerUrl":"https://kplma.org","height":780,"width":600,"useSmartEmbed":false,"isEmbedSupported":false,"display":"card","embedSrc":"https://kplma.org/pdfs/people/Elizabeth%20Sampson.pdf","alt":"","hasEmbedOpenButton":true,"isLiveOnMobile":false,"title":"KPL bio- Elizabeth B. Sampson","description":"A one-page biography on Elizabeth B. Sampson, put together by Kingston Public Library archival staff.","thumbnailResourceId":"r-sYt9fD"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-nlpNCO":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-EphPrI","n-a0gobD","n-d5kmWC","n-leRe0p","n-fH0aPL"]},"n-O4Khlk":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-f1rnfN","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true,"attribution":"Photo credit: Jimmy Powell, Ecology Program Director at Jones River Watershed Association"}},"n-DRxzhO":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-nlpNCO","n-O4Khlk"]},"n-JCALMs":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-y7bKYn":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Bay Circuit Trail (BCT) runs through many habitat types in Sampson Park (see \u003ca href=\"https://www.baycircuit.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.baycircuit.org/\u003c/a\u003e for more info on this eastern MA long-distance trail). Several other trails traverse the property. Only the BCT is blazed at this time, but we have upcoming plans to color-blaze several of these trails, making navigation simpler. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-2PsXYX":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The property is popular with hunters, so be sure to pay attention to hunting seasons, and wear blaze orange or bright colors as appropriate. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-y0bZCU":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"There is fishing access to the north side of Soule\u0026apos;s Pond by the Water Department pumping station, but there\u0026apos;s no parking by the access point on South Street. There was an ADA-compliant fishing deck at Sampson\u0026apos;s Pond, but the pond no longer exists due to the Elm Street Dam removal (more on that in the next section). ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-KAoL35":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-U3zsFL","caption":"All maps are available at \u003ca href=\"https://www.kingstonma.gov/600/Conservation-Properties-Trail-Maps\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.kingstonma.gov/600/Conservation-Properties-Trail-Maps\u003c/a\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-3y9V3X":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-JCALMs","n-y7bKYn","n-2PsXYX","n-y0bZCU","n-KAoL35"]},"n-7zRMic":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-yM3w3q","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-ZeVgqd":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-3y9V3X","n-7zRMic"]},"n-4OP2uu":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026 Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Uh3BL3":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sampson Park consists of a great mosaic of habitat types, from white pine-oak forest to several types of floodplain forest, with patches of American beech, eastern hemlock, ash and hickory in between. The real diversity comes from the new floodplain, created in the former location of Sampson\u0026apos;s Pond. The Elm Street Dam was removed in 2019, following the Wapping Road Dam removal of 2011, allowing river herring to make it upstream to Silver Lake (more accurately to the fish ladder at Forge Pond Dam). While some lament the lack of a pond, Sampson\u0026apos;s Pond was a shallow, warm-water feature that impacted water quality in the Jones River through reduced dissolved oxygen levels, increased water temperature, nutrient loading and excessive aquatic vegetation. The water now flows quickly through, staying cold and oxygenated. One question we often get is \u0026quot;but what about the bass?\u0026quot; The answer is - while they\u0026apos;re fun to fish for, from an ecological perspective we don\u0026apos;t really consider bass beneficial. Largemouth (and smallmouth for that matter) bass are not native to Massachusetts, but the Mississippi River watershed and Great Lakes region. Once introduced to new areas, they dominate quickly, skewing the balance of aquatic species\u0026apos; presence and quantities. For an extreme example of this, look up the extinction of the Atitlan Grebe in Guatemala. While recreation, including fishing, is important, the Conservation Department\u0026apos;s stance is to support the native biodiversity of Kingston. In this case that means restoring the habitat not only for diadromous fish species like American eel, river herring and rainbow smelt, but native coldwater species like eastern brook trout."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-lRXc5q":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"The area of the former pond now functions as a natural floodplain, there to accept flood waters and minimize infrastructure damage during storm events. The floral diversity is amazing, with tons of flowering forbs and shrubs to keep our native pollinators busy. Even more amazing is that all of this sprouted from the natural seed bank in the sediment of the pond bottom, either the result of pre-impoundment seed deposition, or from seeds sinking beneath the pond over the decades.  ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-JUyaPv":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-6XEA5y","caption":"Cardinal flower in bloom, among goldentop, common woolsedge, buttonbush, and pickerelweed. All but one of these are important in supporting pollinators (not to say that woolsedge isn\u0026apos;t important in other ways!)."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-X6eZvt":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-ltDRgS","caption":"Virginia marsh-St. John\u0026apos;s-wort (\u003cem\u003eTriadenum virginicum\u003c/em\u003e) in bloom, in the new floodplain meadow where Sampson\u0026apos;s Pond used to be."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-g5GY2I":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-Iiag9O","caption":"Hollow Joe-Pye weed (\u003cem\u003eEutrochium fistulosum\u003c/em\u003e), another important pollinator plant found in the new floodplain meadow running along the Jones River."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-rHwCaJ":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-4OP2uu","n-Uh3BL3","n-lRXc5q","n-JUyaPv","n-X6eZvt","n-g5GY2I"]},"n-ydtXMg":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-IW0XVS","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fill","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-sGD4fr":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-rHwCaJ","n-ydtXMg"]},"n-cbwHB7":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Silver Lake Sanctuary","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-PPI4Nn":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"General Information","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-CaPXdi":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"bullet-list","text":"\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSize:\u003c/strong\u003e 104 acres\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocation \u0026amp; Access:\u003c/strong\u003e Take dirt road Barse\u0026apos;s Lane south off Rte 27 (Pembroke Street) to a small dirt parking area behind our kiosk- fits between 5 and 10 vehicles. Trails lead in 3 directions from the parking area. Please be sure to stay off of the private property to the S/SE of the parking area. There may be room for one car to park at the Bay Circuit Trail access point off Sheridan Drive, but you\u0026apos;ll be parking right next to a residential home.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHistory:\u003c/strong\u003e Purchased by the Town of Kingston for conservation in 1997, with help from the state Self-Help Program and the City of Brockton, who purchased a Conservation Restriction on the original 91.4 acres. Brockton had a vested interest in maintaining Silver Lake water quality, as it has relied on it as a municipal water source for roughly 120 years. Unfortunately, in recent years their reliance has increased, to the detriment of water quality and ecological health (see the Ecology section for more information). The property once supported a cranberry growing operation, starting as early as 1935 and running until Town purchase. Cranberry infrastructure can still be seen throughout the property, from a small pumphouse to water control structures, reservoirs and ditches. \u003c/li\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-SNEgO1":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Silver Lake has seen much over its history, being of glacial origin- a remnant of the massive Glacial Lake Taunton, now the 12th largest natural lake in the Commonwealth. Being a historically significant fish run with abundant freshwater mussels, the shores of this lake have been used by indigenous groups for nearly 15,000 years. The colonial period brought changes from the 18th to 19th centuries, including the construction of water-powered sawmill, forge, anchor works, nail and tack factories where the Jones River exits the lake. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-zP0U03":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Silver Lake itself has supported various industries as well. In 1751, iron ore was found in the lake, and about 3,000 tons were taken from the lake bottom. The important commercial ice business of the 19th century caused its name to be changed from Jones River Pond to Silver Lake, invoking images of shining, immaculately clean water to be harvested and sold as ice blocks. This business shipped ice as far as the eastern Caribbean island of Martinique beginning in 1805 and continued selling ice through 1870. Up until Brockton\u0026apos;s state-sponsored takeover of Silver Lake in 1898, it was a vibrant regional recreation destination, connected by train to the greater Boston area, with sailboats and steamers connecting travelers to the beauty of the lake and surrounding forest.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-F5L4e7":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Over the last fifty years residential development has pressured the shoreline- a review of imagery from 1960 shows a mostly intact forested buffer around the lake, save a few cranberry bogs and other agricultural uses. Over recent decades, large portions of shoreline have been developed for housing, though most of these developments have been built according to state and local wetlands protection regulations. Still, the development pressure, and the detrimental impacts it causes on water bodies, highlights the importance of preserving property like this one. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-gdePxo":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-cbwHB7","n-PPI4Nn","n-CaPXdi","n-SNEgO1","n-zP0U03","n-F5L4e7"]},"n-pO51rW":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-fK6XOr","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-8U10TE":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-gdePxo","n-pO51rW"]},"n-dLMzcv":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Recreation","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-ygSWTh":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The long-distance Bay Circuit Trail passes through Silver Lake Sanctuary from Sheridan Drive by trail to the parking area, and by a dirt driveway from there out to Rte 27 before heading into Pembroke. From the parking area you can head east or west on pedestrian trails (avoid going S/SE onto private property, heed the signs). To the west (past an old decorative iron gate), the trail leads past an old retired cranberry bog (can loop around it as well) and reaches a clearing at Silver Lake, technically in the Town of Pembroke. There\u0026apos;s also a parallel trail that has a bench at a lookout over the lake, a great peaceful spot to sit. Headed east from the parking area, past an old barn, trails lead around and through wetlands where cranberry agriculture once dominated, and infrastructure can still be seen, such as water control structures. A trail leads left out to Barbara Road, or right to the lake, where an earthen dike separated the bogs and lake. There is a trail along this dike, but it\u0026apos;s a bit overgrown, and not shown on this map."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-x4fIoN":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Silver Lake Sanctuary is a great place to hike, walk your dog (leashes required as well as cleaning up after them), watch birds, or naturalize.  Consider using eBird or iNaturalist to document your sightings, it helps us track the biodiversity of our properties!","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-Apn0hq":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-dLMzcv","n-ygSWTh","n-x4fIoN"]},"n-P3A0l5":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-oYHCnR","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-aW4OQQ":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-Apn0hq","n-P3A0l5"]},"n-aWdtyZ":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Ecology \u0026 Natural History","type":"h3"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-OKnjij":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Silver Lake Sanctuary is a diverse property, containing white pine-oak forest, lakefront, streams, at least one vernal pool, and several other wetland types. This diversity makes it a great site for birdwatching, from winter resident waterfowl, to raptors, to seasonal migrant songbirds stopping over for a meal before continuing on to faraway places. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-4hodvv":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-sobSkR","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":false,"link":"https://www.inaturalist.org/photos/169651176","attribution":"c. Becky Matsubara through iNaturalist - \u003ca href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/beckymatsubara/26794388798/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.flickr.com/photos/beckymatsubara/26794388798/\u003c/a\u003e","caption":"Hermit Thrush, \u003cem\u003eCatharus guttatus\u003c/em\u003e, a summer resident of SLS that winters throughout the southern U.S. to Central America. They have one of the most interesting songs in the bird world. To hear an example, see: \u003ca href=\"https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Hermit_Thrush/sounds\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Hermit_Thrush/sounds\u003c/a\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-wVHjUu":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"What makes this property the most ecologically important is the lake itself. Some incorrectly call it a reservoir- in actuality it is the 12th largest natural lake in the Commonwealth, formed by the receding of the glaciers during the last ice age, a remnant of the massive Glacial Lake Taunton. For roughly 15,000 years, the lake has spilled over into the Jones River, which connects the 640-acre lake to the Atlantic Ocean via Kingston Bay. Diadromous fish species evolved to take advantage of this system. This includes the catadromous American eel and the anadromous river herring (alewife and blueback herring), although in precolonial times there were likely additional species no longer found here, including Atlantic salmon and American shad. For more information on diadromous fish, see \u003ca href=\"https://seatuck.org/diadromous-fish/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://seatuck.org/diadromous-fish/\u003c/a\u003e.","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-oU86e5":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"These fisheries were a treasured resource from precolonial through colonial times. Dams installed to power mills or other works were seasonal- the water power was used when needed, and the blockage removed in time to let the fish pass. Only through the industrial revolution did this practice shift towards permanent dams, interrupting the life cycle of these fish and beginning regional declines and extirpations. As the scientific community began touting the benefits of removing remnant dams, Jones River Watershed Association (JRWA) and the Conservation Department began planning for the restoration of the Jones River fish runs. The Wapping Road Dam was removed in 2011, and the Elm Street Dam in 2019, allowing connectivity between the ocean and the Forge Pond Dam, though not to Silver Lake yet. A temporary fish ladder was installed, though herring counts remained close to zero for several years, while most regional numbers also faultered. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-LSEnHo":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"In the spring of 2024, roughly 24,000 alewife made it into Silver Lake to breed, through the fish ladder and by the efforts of JRWA, the Conservation Department, and MA Division of Marine Fisheries (DMF). Based on the low end of the scientific records of clutch size, assuming half of these fish were female, that means roughly 600 million eggs were laid. Tens to hundreds of millions of juvenile herring  began growing in Silver Lake, preparing for their upcoming trip to the ocean, where they would provide meaningful forage for Gulf of Maine fish stocks. Unfortunately they have not made it there as of this writing (Dec 2024). Due to a drought year and Brockton\u0026apos;s increased reliance on the lake (~10 million gallons per day taken on average, with no other source used), the lake was down as low as minus 52.7\u0026quot; below level, and has not connected to the Jones since June. In the meantime, on calm, warm days you can see the juvenile herring patrolling the shallows along the ever-receding shoreline, looking for the exit that never appears. They are preyed upon by yellow perch and nonnative bass, and it\u0026apos;s unclear whether the zooplankton they feed on will survive through the winter without their population crashing due to the heavy pressure. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-XrvrgS":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-eUuXCE","caption":"River herring (Alewife - \u003cem\u003eAlosa pseudoharengus\u003c/em\u003e) schooling beneath the spillway at Forge Pond Dam, spring 2024. Roughly 24,000 of them were able to access Silver Lake to breed, though their cohort of juveniles has been unable to leave.               "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-XLuK6c":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-HqnJVq","caption":"Oct 31, 2024- standing at the new receded shoreline of Silver Lake, looking towards where the outlet to the Jones River should be. The patch of cattails at the center of the photo should be the outlet, which has not flowed since sometime in June of 2024. Juvenile alewife and adult American eels cannot access the ocean."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-cx6LwX":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"In addition to the herring issue, DMF monitors American eels in the lake through trapping and PIT-tagging efforts. Fully adult males and females were captured, many being of the size you would expect to migrate to the Sargasso Sea to breed, a few even beginning to turn silver as they do when ready for the journey (they also stop eating at this point, their digestive system shrinking in favor of expanding reproductive organs). Several were repeat-captured between 2023 and 2024, indicating that they were not able to leave. Certainly in late 2024 they have not had the opportunity to do so. Since the latest scientific understanding is that they breed communally, it would be no use for any of them to leave in winter or spring, while the rest of the species arrives in the Sargasso in fall.   ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-U4XLpl":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-cn3rBT","caption":"A large female American eel (Anguilla rostrata) trapped and measured in September 2024 by MA DMF. This individual was PIT-tagged here in 2023 and has not left to breed, though it\u0026apos;s clearly large enough to do so. The lack of a stable outlet to the Jones River is a likely cause for delay in changing body morphology in preparation for the long journey to the Sargasso Sea. Large females like this are crucial to the survival of the species due to the amount of eggs they\u0026apos;re capable of producing. She\u0026apos;s likely to be trapped again in 2025, unless she is predated.         "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-NUIc04":{"type":"video","data":{"video":"r-RC1MVj","thumbnail":"r-W6PQFj","caption":"DMF releasing several large eels after processing. American eels exhibit strong sexual dimorphism, with mature females severely outsizing males. The two very small individuals that leave the bucket at the end are males, the rest are females. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-W6lCaX":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Drawdowns from Brockton\u0026apos;s continued reliance on Silver Lake have had detrimental effects on freshwater mussels as well. The winter of 2016-2017 combined drought with relentless municipal water use, to the point where the lake was 100 inches below level (over 8 feet low). An estimated millions of mussels died due to cold exposure and predation, including at least one state-listed species (multiple state-listed species were reported here, but at greater depths). In fall/winter of 2024-2025, many mussels have perished due to exposure, including eastern floater, eastern lampmussel, eastern elliptio, and the state-listed tidewater mucket. As of this writing in Dec 2024, lake levels are still more than three feet below level, and have been as low as 52.7\u0026quot; below level within the last month. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-klA8tr":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-G2xuxZ","caption":"January, 2017: Estimated millions of freshwater mussels perish from drawdown exposure due to Brockton\u0026apos;s municipal water use. This includes at least one state-listed species, the tidewater mucket (\u003cem\u003eAtlanticoncha ochracea\u003c/em\u003e).                              "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-sx9gJn":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-68Oggo","caption":"An eastern floater (\u003cem\u003ePyganodon cataracta\u003c/em\u003e), exposed due to drawdown by the City of Brockton, October 31, 2024. This individual was relocated to deeper water before dessication or predation took its life. Gulls and other large birds took advantage of the exposure, predating many exposed mussels by dropping them on the rocks or simply pecking through their carapaces. These mussels are crucial in maintaining clean water, and can live for many decades, possibly over one hundred years. For more info, see \u003ca href=\"https://www.xerces.org/endangered-species/freshwater-mussels/about\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.xerces.org/endangered-species/freshwater-mussels/about\u003c/a\u003e"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-y9RZBR":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-bbacGO","caption":"Though typically sedentary, some species of freshwater mussel are capable of long movements. Here you can see the \u0026quot;footsteps\u0026quot; of an eastern floater that is attempt-ing to stay submerged as the water level steadily dropped above it. This was taken in October of 2024, and this behavior was observed all over the shoreline, though many individuals were either unable to move, or move enough to survive."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-CmJbax":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"In addition to the impacts from drawdown, water quality in Silver Lake has suffered due to Brockton\u0026apos;s diversion of water from Monponsett Pond in Halifax, for the purposes of maintaining Silver Lake\u0026apos;s water level, and to prevent flooding issues for homes around Monponsett Pond. Monponsett Pond has long been known for poor water quality, including dangerous cyanobacteria blooms. Lowering Silver Lake and then filling it back up with impaired water has led Silver Lake itself to be 303d listed by the EPA as an \u0026quot;Impaired Water Body.\u0026quot;"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-aWV6lQ":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-8Va52o","caption":"September 14, 2023: JRWA documents a dark plume entering Silver Lake through Brockton\u0026apos;s Monponsett diversion pipe. The dark coloration is thought to have been from the 1964-installed pipe breaching somewhere in the Peterson Swamp, scouring muck sediment. While the dark color has ceased, diversion continues to affect water quality, especially when combined with heavy withdrawals by Brockton."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-J6O21T":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-aWdtyZ","n-OKnjij","n-4hodvv","n-wVHjUu","n-oU86e5","n-LSEnHo","n-XrvrgS","n-XLuK6c","n-cx6LwX","n-U4XLpl","n-NUIc04","n-W6lCaX","n-klA8tr","n-sx9gJn","n-y9RZBR","n-CmJbax","n-aWV6lQ"]},"n-6tkmHB":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-Dj96Yt","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-ge8dzu":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-J6O21T","n-6tkmHB"]},"n-Iylaag":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Triphammer Park \u0026amp; Jones River Wildlife Preserve","type":"h2"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-eaijYw":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Triphammer Park"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-SfyV7L":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Triphammer Park is a small site, at only a half-acre, with parking for one car across the street from 27 Wapping Road (use caution, depending on vehicle size you may need to back out into Rte 106, which is not safe). There is a short trail leading to the Jones River, shown in blue on the map to the right. At the river, there is a large green ash tree at the river\u0026apos;s edge, one that will hopefully be able to fend off the invasive emerald ash borer that has removed ash from the landscape across the U.S. over the last decade. Looking north towards Rte 106, you\u0026apos;ll see the site of the former Wapping Road Dam, removed in 2011 to allow passage for river herring and American eels. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-mhz5XX":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"In the video posted below from Jones River Ecology on YouTube, you\u0026apos;ll see juvenile eels attempting to scale the concrete dam wall on their way upstream (1:30-3:30). With the dam gone, and especially since the Elm Street Dam was removed in 2019 as well, these and other fish have direct access from the Forge Pond Dam near the outlet at Silver Lake all the way to Kingston Bay and the Gulf of Maine. ","type":"paragraph"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-fcRC4k":{"type":"embed","data":{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3RrS4VoV4ZI","embedType":"video","title":"Wapping Road Dam Story","description":"Jones River in Kingston, MA was named after the Captain of the Mayflower, Christopher Jones. The pilgrims explored the Jones River and learned from the natives how to take advantage of the plentiful fish. Through colonial times the river was used for fishing, pasture land, water supplies, and to power small mills and forges.","thumbnailUrl":"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/3RrS4VoV4ZI/hqdefault.jpg","providerUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/","height":480,"width":854,"caption":"Wapping Road Dam Story","isEmbedSupported":true,"display":"inline","embedSrc":"https://cdn.embedly.com/widgets/media.html?a=0\u0026src=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fembed%2F3RrS4VoV4ZI%3Ffeature%3Doembed\u0026display_name=YouTube\u0026url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fwatch%3Fv%3D3RrS4VoV4ZI\u0026image=https%3A%2F%2Fi.ytimg.com%2Fvi%2F3RrS4VoV4ZI%2Fhqdefault.jpg\u0026type=text%2Fhtml\u0026schema=youtube"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-YPFK9A":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"This site has a tremendous historical record, as you\u0026apos;ll see from the interpretive panel at the trailhead. A Wampanoag fish weir existed in this area according to colonial records, used to take advantage of the shallow nature of the river at this location and the seasonal abundance of fish seen in precolonial times. Mill priveleges were established here in the mid-17th century, and the dam was used for industry until 1980. Through the centuries it was used to power sawmills, tools, anchors, gristmills, tack and nails, cotton goods, and most recently to make woolen goods."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-EnFlhp":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-2js27R","caption":"1885- A bird\u0026apos;s eye view of Triphammer, looking northwest. The Holmes anchor forge is at center, with a dam to its left (from Technical Memorandum for Wapping Road Dam removal, Public Archaeology Laboratory, January 15, 2013).                "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-eB2awL":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"h3","text":"Jones River Wildlife Preserve"},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-rIimKI":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"This 9.65-acre parcel was deeded to the Conservation Commission in 2019 as part of permit requirements for development under the RDEOS zoning regulations (Residential Development Encouraging Open Space) for the Jones River Estates subdivision. The intent was to prevent the development from negatively affecting the Jones River by preserving an intact river buffer. There is a small gravel parking area between the two stormwater retention basins, with a pedestrian trail leading back into the woods, by the river, and out to the northern portion of Captain Jones Way. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-uFMPgj":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":""},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-l8pYuH":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-Iylaag","n-eaijYw","n-SfyV7L","n-mhz5XX","n-fcRC4k","n-YPFK9A","n-EnFlhp","n-eB2awL","n-rIimKI","n-uFMPgj"]},"n-LK72nv":{"type":"expressmap","data":{"map":"r-rvVkhV","basemap":"worldImagery","alt":"Interactive map showing the location of Triphammer Park and Jones River Wildlife Preserve, along with applicable parking areas and trails (blue lines)"}},"n-zEHKdh":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-l8pYuH","n-LK72nv"]},"n-Zrt4u1":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"Where this trail exits to the road, there is an area that was cleared during the development process, but lies within the conservation property. This area will be converted into a pollinator meadow rather than regrowing into forest, as this will increase local biodiversity. Drought conditions in the first couple years have hindered progress, but in time this will be a great area for observing butterflies, bumblebees and other pollinator species. "},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-YfGGfA":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-cOHujV","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true,"attribution":"Adult American lady photo modified (cropped) from iNaturalist photo 2124214, taken by Susan Elliott \u003ca href=\"https://www.inaturalist.org/photos/2124214\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.inaturalist.org/photos/2124214\u003c/a\u003e","caption":"This host plant - larva combination was discovered during pre-work biodiversity surveys of the pollinator meadow site at Jones River Wildlife Preserve. The native wildflower sweet everlasting is a larval hostplant for the American lady butterfly. Over time, this meadow will establish with a diversity of native wildflowers and grasses, providing more floral resources than the surrounding pine-oak forest."},"config":{"size":"wide"}},"n-swPB57":{"type":"immersive-narrative-panel","data":{"panelStyle":"themed"},"children":["n-Zrt4u1","n-YfGGfA"]},"n-ZT02O2":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-eEkB9l","isInFullView":false,"isExpandable":true},"config":{"placement":{"type":"fit","fill":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5},"fit":{"color":"backgroundColor"}}}},"n-ej6ZxJ":{"type":"immersive-slide","data":{"transition":"fade"},"children":["n-swPB57","n-ZT02O2"]},"n-Bqc1QY":{"type":"immersive","data":{"type":"sidecar","subtype":"docked-panel","narrativePanelPosition":"start","narrativePanelSize":"medium"},"children":["n-uLlUhk","n-2RXNZh","n-JhwGiK","n-4Je7vy","n-v2fMue","n-JWjDi8","n-CqnvIk","n-WR2DO2","n-DmDtuA","n-fYgkRD","n-JzH6mM","n-rKwRpB","n-TZdpcH","n-162fgk","n-ZUyFLc","n-WYhaFH","n-NPtbHN","n-Nbgy18","n-v964AE","n-XI9NSv","n-B4vrRz","n-KNbmtB","n-rulkJd","n-B8Wyqn","n-PF8r2d","n-t9flpq","n-TVdxy1","n-chx1lK","n-QDcEyj","n-2f1XKr","n-Nbsyvg","n-1bZI9M","n-VdiEvv","n-V04KfX","n-he74Dt","n-bxWFVO","n-DRxzhO","n-ZeVgqd","n-sGD4fr","n-8U10TE","n-aW4OQQ","n-ge8dzu","n-zEHKdh","n-ej6ZxJ"]},"n-IZ4vfS":{"type":"text","data":{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Kingston Conservation Department relies almost entirely on volunteer efforts to keep our properties maintained. Every bit helps, from physical labor (pruning, digging sign posts, picking up trash) to simply walking sites and letting us know what you see. Please consider joining our Volunteer Stewardship Program if you value open space and want to lend a hand. See \u003ca href=\"https://www.kingstonma.gov/691/Kingston-Land-Steward-Program\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003ehttps://www.kingstonma.gov/691/Kingston-Land-Steward-Program\u003c/a\u003e for more information. Thanks!!"}},"n-rrq4Rj":{"type":"image","data":{"image":"r-6P906B","caption":"QR code leading to our Land Stewards Program"}},"n-va3gyR":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"h4"}},"n-J5ozml":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"","type":"paragraph"}},"n-GV13lc":{"type":"attribution","data":{"content":"","attribution":""}},"n-O7DiJ0":{"type":"credits","children":["n-va3gyR","n-J5ozml","n-GV13lc"]},"n-qIWivp":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"Created by the Kingston Conservation Department, December 2024","type":"h4","textAlignment":"center"}},"n-2UXJxu":{"type":"text","data":{"text":"To report any errors, or issues with information stated here, email mpenella@kingstonma.gov","type":"paragraph","textAlignment":"center"}},"n-k5fwCv":{"type":"attribution","data":{"content":"","attribution":""}},"n-qamuzg":{"type":"credits","children":["n-qIWivp","n-2UXJxu","n-k5fwCv"]},"n-7QYGwL":{"type":"story","data":{"storyTheme":"r-tLDD2R"},"config":{"coverDate":"last-published","isSocialIconsHidden":false,"shouldHideSimilarItems":true,"storyLocale":"en"},"children":[